Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 09:54:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Document the session and cookie contract of citomni/http

The Session reference, the Flash reference and the controller how-to now describe the contract of the matching citomni/http version: Cookie owns the attributes of every cookie, Session owns storage and id mechanics, and reads never create a session.

Why:
- The Session reference documented options that citomni/http removed and now refuses (session.rotate_interval, session.fingerprint), the reserved keys _sess_rotated_at and _sess_fpr, a Secure fallback to raw server variables, and a regenerate() that required an active session. It said that every read starts the session.
- The Flash reference described ensureBags() and a session start on every use. The controller how-to said that reads start the session, that rotation could run on a timer, and that Cookie derives its defaults from the http config, which included the domain inferred from http.base_url.

What changed:
- reference/session-service.md is rewritten for the new contract: Ownership between Cookie, Session and Auth; the session.* and cookie.* baselines; the precedence of session.cookie_* over cfg.cookie; the Secure inference; validation; storage retention and garbage collection, with the Debian and Ubuntu cron caveat; the save path; refused settings; the removed options; the method reference; reads and session creation; the startup pipeline; rotation under concurrency; failure semantics; the interplay with citomni/authenticate; and upgrade notes.
- reference/flash-service.md: Reads never create a session, missing bags read as empty, Flash uses only get(), set() and remove(), and the troubleshooting row points at the session cookie.
- how-to/controllers-authoring-and-usage.md: The Session section describes reads, destroy() and rotation as they are now; the Cookie section describes host-only defaults, the Secure inference, refused headers and attributes().

Deployment:
- Publish together with the citomni/http version that makes cookies host-only and stops creating sessions on reads.

